`{{predicate-name}} : {{domain}} × {{range}} → Bool`

## When to use instance-of vs has-meta-type vs extends

| Predicate | Levels involved |
|---|---|
| `instance-of` | individual → class (e.g., a specific memorial → `:mm/Decision`) |
| `has-meta-type` | class → metaclass (e.g., `:mm/Decision` → `:dt/Class`) |
| `extends` | class → superclass (e.g., `:mm/Decision` → `:mm/Memory`) |

## Audit invariants

- "instance-of points to a declared class" — every `instance-of:` target must be a `:dt/Class` instance
- "type hierarchy consistency" — `instance-of` and `extends` must be consistent (no orphan classes)

## Formal ontology mappings

Aligned predicates from established formal vocabularies:

- **RDF** `rdf:type` — exact semantic match (the canonical instance-class predicate in RDF)
- **OWL** `owl:NamedIndividual` (in conjunction with class assertion) — close analogue
- **RDFS** — uses `rdf:type` directly (RDFS does not introduce a separate instance-class predicate)
- **DC Terms** `dcterms:type` — close analogue (literal-typed)
- **SKOS** `skos:inScheme` — distant analogue (concept-instance-of-scheme)
- **PROV-O** — no direct equivalent (PROV-O uses `rdf:type` for class assertions)

